## The briefs, recovered

The dispatches behind this paper ran under briefs, and the briefs were written by the hub. That makes the brief files part of the apparatus of the record rather than commentary on it: they are the texts the workers actually received, and a reader who wants to reproduce the record has to be able to put hands on them. For most of the project's life that was not possible, because the brief files were not in the companion repository. They entered it on 14 October 2026, when 24 of them were recovered from the hub's archive and committed under briefs/. The recovered set consists of 23 v1 dispatched briefs and one shared round-2 reviewer note, which is the only file in the folder that is not a dispatched brief. Two further briefs that the record accounts for were not retained, so the archive holds 24 of the 26 briefs the record knows of, and the reader should keep that arithmetic in mind when the counts are examined more closely below.

The first obstacle to reading briefs/ is that filenames do not mean what they appear to mean. The six section briefs carry permuted filenames: the brief for section 3b is not named 3b, and navigating by name will misattribute briefs to sections. What makes the folder navigable is briefs/README.md, which maps every instrument row to its brief, its section, its needle and the retained output file. The README is the only reliable index into the folder, and the working procedure follows from that: take an instrument row, follow its mapping to the brief, the section, the needle and the output, and treat any identification made from a filename alone as unsupported.

The second anchor sits inside the briefs themselves. Every dispatched brief ends with a trailer naming its needle. The needle is therefore carried in the very text the worker receives, and this is what made the v1 echo problem matter: wherever brief text could reappear in a worker's returned output, the needle would reappear with it. For a reproducer, the trailer is the checkpoint. The needle named in a brief's trailer should agree with the needle the README's row assigns to that brief, and the retained output filed against that row should be read with that three-way correspondence in view: row, trailer, output.

The edges of the archive need to be stated plainly, because they are exactly where a reproduction attempt will stumble. Two one-line briefs were not retained. The first connectivity probe is lost outright: there is no file and no reconstruction, and nothing in the repository supports any claim about its text. The brief of the first live v2 dispatch survives, but only as a reconstruction, built from the author's record of the command that created it. The reconstruction is 87 bytes, matching the row, and it is hub-attested. The command itself is not retained, so the evidence for that brief stops at the author's record and the hub's attestation: a reader can confirm the byte count against the row and can record the attestation, but the text cannot be verified from the repository. Beyond that reconstruction the folder holds no v2 dispatched briefs at all; the dispatched material in the archive is v1.

What the recovered briefs say, and do not say, about worker constraints is as important to reproduction as the mapping. The 13 drafting briefs carried no action constraint of any kind: no write restriction, no directory, no tool limit. Nothing in those briefs told a drafting worker where it could write or what it could use. Scope and single-file writes were instructed only in the nine review briefs, which were dispatched ten times, and which came after a worker wrote directly into the manuscript, the incident the record names INC-001. The constraint regime visible in this folder is therefore not a design constant of the project. It is a reaction, dated by the record to after INC-001, and a reproducer reconstructing the protocol should date the appearance of constraints from that incident rather than projecting them backwards over the drafting phase.

Two counts around the review briefs remain open in the record as reproduced here, and this section will not close them by inference. Thirteen drafting briefs plus nine review briefs account for 22 of the 23 v1 dispatched briefs the archive holds, and the facts available to this section do not classify the remaining file. The phrase "dispatched ten times" is likewise not reconciled anywhere in this section with nine brief files; how the ten dispatches distribute across the nine retained briefs is not stated. These gaps are properties of the recovered record, not of this summary, and a reader walking the folder row by row should expect to meet them and leave them open unless some other part of the record closes them.

One brief is the site of the second named incident and needs separate treatment. The brief for the constraints section asked for the needle-hit rate as its worked example, and it permitted "concrete examples from a live hub-and-worker system". The worker had no record of any such system. What it produced instead were invented figures for 147 briefs, filed in the record as INC-004. No set of 147 briefs exists anywhere in the archive described in this section, and the count enters the record only inside the invented figures; this section cannot say more about where it came from, because the record does not. The division of responsibility the record draws should be quoted exactly: the invention was the worker's, and the licence was the hub's. Both halves of that sentence are checkable, which is what makes it useful. The licence sits in the brief itself and can be read in the folder. The invented figures are not in the archive; they are in the output produced under that brief. The seam between them, a permission granted for a system the worker had no record of, is the precise point at which INC-004 became possible.

Read as a whole, the folder supports a specific kind of verification and stops at identifiable edges. A reproducer can use the README as the index, check each instrument row against its brief's trailer needle and its retained output, date the constraint regime from INC-001, and read the licence clause behind INC-004 in the recovered brief. What cannot be done from the repository alone: the first connectivity probe cannot be examined at all; the brief of the first live v2 dispatch can be checked only for its 87-byte size against the row and for the fact of the hub's attestation, the command that created it having not been retained; and the recovery itself is fixed here only as 14 October 2026, 24 files, under briefs/, with no commit hash given, no description of the hub's archive beyond the fact of the recovery, and no stated verification procedure for the recovery. Those are the honest boundaries of this folder. Everything inside them can be walked row by row against the instrument; everything outside them is attested rather than held.

AB-S4-OK
